1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:07,608
High on the Tibetan Plateau, in a desert basin in western China, a bare hill stands beside a salt lake.

2
00:00:07,608 --> 00:00:12,199
On the signs here, one side of it is called the alien relic site.

3
00:00:12,666 --> 00:00:17,537
Inside a cave in that hill, rusty tubes come straight out of the rock.

4
00:00:17,537 --> 00:00:19,973
Some are as wide as a dinner plate.

5
00:00:19,973 --> 00:00:22,199
Some are as thin as a toothpick.

6
00:00:22,666 --> 00:00:27,933
More of them poke out of the sand on the lake shore, and even out of the water.

7
00:00:27,933 --> 00:00:30,866
Online, they are known as the Baigong pipes.

8
00:00:31,333 --> 00:00:34,210
And the claim attached to them is enormous.

9
00:00:34,210 --> 00:00:40,166
Iron pipes, a hundred and fifty thousand years old, built by visitors from another world.

10
00:00:44,633 --> 00:00:47,612
So tonight, we run this like a detective case.

11
00:00:47,612 --> 00:00:50,268
Six suspects could have made these pipes.

12
00:00:50,268 --> 00:00:54,866
We question every one, check every alibi, and see who is left standing.

13
00:00:55,333 --> 00:00:56,036
The scene.

14
00:00:56,036 --> 00:01:07,500
Lake Toson, a salt lake about forty kilometres southwest of the city of Delingha, in Qinghai province, more than two thousand eight hundred metres above sea level.

15
00:01:07,966 --> 00:01:12,031
It has a twin, a freshwater lake joined to it by a river.

16
00:01:12,031 --> 00:01:14,670
Locals call the pair the lover lakes.

17
00:01:14,670 --> 00:01:22,799
On the shore of the salt lake stands a rocky hill, fifty to sixty metres high, that reports describe as a pyramid.

18
00:01:23,266 --> 00:01:28,111
The first witness is a writer, Bai Yu, who had once worked in geology.

19
00:01:28,111 --> 00:01:34,133
In June nineteen ninety-six, he walked into a triangular cave at the foot of that hill.

20
00:01:34,599 --> 00:01:43,404
Deep inside, a rusty tube came out of the rock wall, fitted so tightly into the stone that it had no visible start and no visible end.

21
00:01:43,404 --> 00:01:48,266
He chipped off a sample and sent it to a state smelting works for testing.

22
00:01:48,733 --> 00:01:51,099
The result: mostly common elements.

23
00:01:51,099 --> 00:01:54,344
But about eight percent could not be identified.

24
00:01:54,344 --> 00:01:57,318
For Bai Yu, that eight percent was the clue.

25
00:01:57,318 --> 00:02:03,199
In his book, Into the Qaidam, he concluded the pipes were built by visitors from space.

26
00:02:03,666 --> 00:02:09,972
In June two thousand two, China's Xinhua news agency carried the story around the world.

27
00:02:09,972 --> 00:02:17,066
A local official told reporters that someone had suggested the site might be an alien launch tower.

28
00:02:17,533 --> 00:02:19,402
So here is the line-up.

29
00:02:19,402 --> 00:02:21,516
Suspect one, the visitors.

30
00:02:21,516 --> 00:02:24,767
Two, a lost civilisation of iron smiths.

31
00:02:24,767 --> 00:02:26,556
Three, modern workers.

32
00:02:26,556 --> 00:02:27,856
Four, a volcano.

33
00:02:27,856 --> 00:02:28,994
Five, a flood.

34
00:02:28,994 --> 00:02:30,457
And six, a forest.

35
00:02:30,457 --> 00:02:31,433
Let's begin.

36
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:34,628
Suspect one has the most famous profile.

37
00:02:34,628 --> 00:02:38,174
A starship lands on the flat ground behind the hill.

38
00:02:38,174 --> 00:02:45,199
Its crew drives pipes into the rock, maybe to pump water from the lake, maybe to anchor a launch tower.

39
00:02:45,666 --> 00:02:47,344
There's even a motive.

40
00:02:47,344 --> 00:02:50,091
The sky here is high, dry and clear.

41
00:02:50,091 --> 00:02:57,567
A big radio telescope of China's Purple Mountain Observatory stands about seventy kilometres away.

42
00:02:57,567 --> 00:03:00,999
If astronomers love this sky, why not aliens?

43
00:03:01,466 --> 00:03:04,972
And the physical evidence the visitors left behind?

44
00:03:04,972 --> 00:03:07,654
That eight percent of unknown material.

45
00:03:07,654 --> 00:03:11,366
Perhaps, the theory says, an alloy from another world.

46
00:03:11,833 --> 00:03:13,291
Time to interrogate.

47
00:03:13,291 --> 00:03:25,099
In May two thousand one, a field team led by geologist Zheng Jiandong, of China's earthquake administration, went to the lake and brought samples back to Beijing.

48
00:03:25,566 --> 00:03:33,464
A senior engineer at a Beijing geology institute compared the pipes' trace elements with meteorites, and with moon rock.

49
00:03:33,464 --> 00:03:34,846
They matched neither.

50
00:03:34,846 --> 00:03:38,466
Whatever these pipes are, they did not come from space.

51
00:03:38,933 --> 00:03:41,178
And the mysterious eight percent?

52
00:03:41,178 --> 00:03:46,961
Tested again, it turned out to be ordinary elements: potassium, aluminium and sodium.

53
00:03:46,961 --> 00:03:50,500
Some of the commonest elements in the Earth's crust.

54
00:03:50,966 --> 00:03:55,019
The visitors' only physical evidence has evaporated.

55
00:03:55,019 --> 00:04:00,162
And no one has ever found a landing pad, a tool, a bolt or a weld.

56
00:04:00,162 --> 00:04:02,033
Suspect one: eliminated.

57
00:04:02,500 --> 00:04:05,016
Suspect two is more down to earth.

58
00:04:05,016 --> 00:04:13,900
Maybe an unknown human culture smelted iron here long before history began, and these pipes are all that survived of it.

59
00:04:14,366 --> 00:04:16,803
The interrogation starts with a date.

60
00:04:16,803 --> 00:04:30,566
Using thermoluminescence, which measures when mineral grains were last heated or exposed to light, the team dated the pipe material to roughly one hundred and forty to one hundred and fifty thousand years ago.

61
00:04:31,033 --> 00:04:33,689
Could anyone have been here that early?

62
00:04:33,689 --> 00:04:35,052
Surprisingly, maybe.

63
00:04:35,052 --> 00:04:43,566
A Denisovan jawbone found in a cave on the north-east edge of the plateau is at least a hundred and sixty thousand years old.

64
00:04:44,033 --> 00:04:46,266
But iron is a different story.

65
00:04:46,266 --> 00:04:53,113
The oldest smelted iron anywhere is about four and a half thousand years old, from Anatolia.

66
00:04:53,113 --> 00:04:58,100
In China, iron working begins only around three thousand years ago.

67
00:04:58,566 --> 00:05:07,213
A hundred-and-fifty-thousand-year-old iron pipeline would mean an Iron Age more than thirty times older than any other.

68
00:05:07,213 --> 00:05:12,300
With no furnace, no slag heap, no tools and no settlement left behind.

69
00:05:12,766 --> 00:05:15,646
And these pipes don't behave like plumbing.

70
00:05:15,646 --> 00:05:25,492
Reports describe them straight, curved, crossing and spindle-shaped, some like sliced melons, some like long cucumbers, some thinner than a pencil.

71
00:05:25,492 --> 00:05:27,100
Suspect two: eliminated.

72
00:05:27,566 --> 00:05:30,417
Suspect three is the sceptic's favourite.

73
00:05:30,417 --> 00:05:32,781
Maybe these are just modern pipes.

74
00:05:32,781 --> 00:05:38,899
Scrap from herders or prospectors, a forgotten water project, or someone having a laugh.

75
00:05:39,366 --> 00:05:40,703
The alibi is strong.

76
00:05:40,703 --> 00:05:47,990
Reports stress that there is no industry here, and that no construction was ever carried out around the lake.

77
00:05:47,990 --> 00:05:51,199
Just a few migrating herders, north of the hill.

78
00:05:51,666 --> 00:05:55,466
More importantly, the tubes are not lying on the rock.

79
00:05:55,466 --> 00:06:00,885
They are inside it, running through solid sandstone and following its layers.

80
00:06:00,885 --> 00:06:03,699
Modern scrap does not grow into bedrock.

81
00:06:04,166 --> 00:06:07,646
And the material is wrong for any factory pipe.

82
00:06:07,646 --> 00:06:09,941
No steel, no welds, no threads.

83
00:06:09,941 --> 00:06:16,899
Tests reported their walls are pyrite and goethite, iron minerals that form naturally in rock.

84
00:06:17,366 --> 00:06:18,317
One footnote.

85
00:06:18,317 --> 00:06:20,364
Humans did damage the scene.

86
00:06:20,364 --> 00:06:27,530
By two thousand one, visitors had broken the first pipe Bai Yu saw and stripped much of the beach.

87
00:06:27,530 --> 00:06:29,065
But making the pipes?

88
00:06:29,065 --> 00:06:30,966
Suspect three: eliminated.

89
00:06:31,433 --> 00:06:35,080
Suspect four comes from inside the science team.

90
00:06:35,080 --> 00:06:45,033
Zheng Jiandong proposed a natural but fiery culprit: iron-rich magma forced up into cracks in the rock as the Tibetan Plateau rose.

91
00:06:45,500 --> 00:06:46,547
He had a clue.

92
00:06:46,547 --> 00:06:49,840
Some of the pipes are unusually radioactive.

93
00:06:49,840 --> 00:06:56,499
Reports say their uranium content is dozens of times higher than in the surrounding rock.

94
00:06:56,966 --> 00:07:00,767
Molten iron squeezed into fissures, cooling into tubes.

95
00:07:00,767 --> 00:07:04,431
It would explain the iron, and perhaps the radiation.

96
00:07:04,431 --> 00:07:09,200
In two thousand seven, he still said this was the option he favoured.

97
00:07:09,666 --> 00:07:14,208
But his colleagues, Zhou De'an and Gao Junping, pushed back hard.

98
00:07:14,208 --> 00:07:22,033
Their samples showed no real evidence of magma, and the rock around the pipes had not been altered by high heat.

99
00:07:22,500 --> 00:07:26,723
The ground here is stable, they argued, with little magma below.

100
00:07:26,723 --> 00:07:32,333
And if there had been volcanic activity, the basin's oil field could not have formed.

101
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:41,325
Neat hollow tubes in layered sand, sitting right beside ordinary sediment, are not what a magma intrusion leaves.

102
00:07:41,325 --> 00:07:43,211
Suspect four: eliminated.

103
00:07:43,211 --> 00:07:46,833
The radiation goes into the file as a loose end.

104
00:07:47,300 --> 00:07:49,017
Suspect five is quieter.

105
00:07:49,017 --> 00:07:58,033
The team noticed something important: the pipes appear only in the harder sandstone, rock laid down by violent ancient floods.

106
00:07:58,500 --> 00:08:03,537
Long before the region dried out, an ancient Lake Toson was far bigger.

107
00:08:03,537 --> 00:08:09,000
Floods poured in, carrying sand, mud and iron, and buried everything quickly.

108
00:08:09,466 --> 00:08:14,719
Once buried and sealed off from the air, the sediment lost its oxygen.

109
00:08:14,719 --> 00:08:22,899
Sulphur compounds in the water broke down into hydrogen sulphide, which reacted with the iron to make pyrite.

110
00:08:22,899 --> 00:08:23,800
Fool's gold.

111
00:08:24,266 --> 00:08:25,937
That fits the chemistry.

112
00:08:25,937 --> 00:08:32,273
The Beijing tests concluded the pipes were a cement of carbon and pyrite, and not man-made.

113
00:08:32,273 --> 00:08:36,033
Exactly what this kind of flood chemistry can produce.

114
00:08:36,500 --> 00:08:39,209
But Zhou De'an raised an objection.

115
00:08:39,209 --> 00:08:44,783
Flood mud alone does not gather iron into neat, hollow, geometric tubes.

116
00:08:44,783 --> 00:08:46,487
Sediment fills spaces.

117
00:08:46,487 --> 00:08:48,267
It doesn't build pipes.

118
00:08:48,267 --> 00:08:50,900
Something gave the iron its shape.

119
00:08:51,366 --> 00:08:57,105
So suspect five had the means, the iron and the chemistry, but not the mould.

120
00:08:57,105 --> 00:08:59,117
Not cleared, not convicted.

121
00:08:59,117 --> 00:09:00,533
Status: accomplice.

122
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:05,320
Which brings us to the last and least likely-looking suspect.

123
00:09:05,320 --> 00:09:06,170
Look around.

124
00:09:06,170 --> 00:09:08,933
In this desert, there's barely a shrub.

125
00:09:08,933 --> 00:09:10,633
Suspect six is a forest.

126
00:09:11,100 --> 00:09:18,365
Gao Junping went back into the field and found something inside the iron tubes: carbonised plant remains.

127
00:09:18,365 --> 00:09:22,033
Charred traces of something that had once been alive.

128
00:09:22,500 --> 00:09:31,766
He cut samples from the inner wall and the outer wall of a pipe in the cave, and sent them to Lanzhou University for a high-precision analysis.

129
00:09:32,233 --> 00:09:33,999
The result had a pattern.

130
00:09:33,999 --> 00:09:39,369
From the inside of the wall outward, iron increases and potassium decreases.

131
00:09:39,369 --> 00:09:45,233
A steady gradient, the kind left when living tissue is slowly replaced by minerals.

132
00:09:45,700 --> 00:09:46,855
Then the clincher.

133
00:09:46,855 --> 00:09:51,541
Many of the pipes show concentric rings, like the growth rings of a tree.

134
00:09:51,541 --> 00:09:55,200
And from the outside, some of them simply look tree-like.

135
00:09:55,666 --> 00:10:06,222
Millions of years ago, the Qaidam Basin was warm and wet, fed by moist air from the Indian Ocean before the Himalayas grew tall enough to block it.

136
00:10:06,222 --> 00:10:07,515
Forests grew here.

137
00:10:07,515 --> 00:10:09,166
And floods buried them.

138
00:10:09,633 --> 00:10:17,148
Buried deep, the soft inner wood rotted away, while iron in the groundwater gathered in the tougher outer layers.

139
00:10:17,148 --> 00:10:23,133
When the core was gone, what remained was a hollow iron tube in the exact shape of a root.

140
00:10:23,600 --> 00:10:27,020
The pipes are hollow because the roots are gone.

141
00:10:27,020 --> 00:10:29,229
They taper because roots taper.

142
00:10:29,229 --> 00:10:32,435
They run in every direction because roots do.

143
00:10:32,435 --> 00:10:35,500
Suspect six, the forest: our prime suspect.

144
00:10:35,966 --> 00:10:37,263
Back to the board.

145
00:10:37,263 --> 00:10:39,063
The visitors, eliminated.

146
00:10:39,063 --> 00:10:41,080
The lost smiths, eliminated.

147
00:10:41,080 --> 00:10:43,025
Modern workers, eliminated.

148
00:10:43,025 --> 00:10:44,753
The volcano, eliminated.

149
00:10:44,753 --> 00:10:46,338
The flood, accomplice.

150
00:10:46,338 --> 00:10:48,066
The forest, the culprit.

151
00:10:48,533 --> 00:10:50,072
It was a two-part job.

152
00:10:50,072 --> 00:10:51,890
The forest made the shape.

153
00:10:51,890 --> 00:10:56,436
The flood and its iron chemistry filled that shape with minerals.

154
00:10:56,436 --> 00:11:00,633
Then the rising plateau lifted the old lake bed into a hill.

155
00:11:01,100 --> 00:11:02,905
And this isn't a one-off.

156
00:11:02,905 --> 00:11:17,133
In nineteen ninety-three, geologists Joann Mossa and Bruce Schumacher described iron-rich cylinders up to seventy centimetres wide in Louisiana soils, and identified them as fossil tree-root casts.

157
00:11:17,600 --> 00:11:27,056
In Utah's Navajo Sandstone, iron oxide pipes several metres long run through the rock, made by ancient groundwater, not engineers.

158
00:11:27,056 --> 00:11:33,166
Geologists have puzzled over stone cylinders like these since the eighteen hundreds.

159
00:11:33,633 --> 00:11:35,778
Even the age makes sense now.

160
00:11:35,778 --> 00:11:43,469
A hundred and fifty thousand years is impossibly old for a smelter, but perfectly ordinary for a fossil.

161
00:11:43,469 --> 00:11:47,833
The date that sounded alien was actually a geological clue.

162
00:11:48,299 --> 00:11:50,478
Bai Yu was right about one thing.

163
00:11:50,478 --> 00:11:52,393
These are not ordinary rocks.

164
00:11:52,393 --> 00:11:58,666
He just looked up at the sky for the answer, when it was buried in the ground beneath his feet.

165
00:11:59,133 --> 00:12:02,315
A good detective admits what is still open.

166
00:12:02,315 --> 00:12:03,869
First, the radiation.

167
00:12:03,869 --> 00:12:10,899
Why some pipes carry so much uranium has never been fully explained in any study we could find.

168
00:12:11,366 --> 00:12:12,721
Second, the paperwork.

169
00:12:12,721 --> 00:12:16,845
Most of what we know comes from news reports and magazine articles.

170
00:12:16,845 --> 00:12:23,433
The lab data have never been published in an international journal where other scientists could check them.

171
00:12:23,899 --> 00:12:25,305
And third, the timing.

172
00:12:25,305 --> 00:12:32,910
Thermoluminescence dates the minerals, and the pipes could have formed over a long stretch after the trees were buried.

173
00:12:32,910 --> 00:12:35,466
One number doesn't tell the whole story.

174
00:12:35,933 --> 00:12:38,317
Meanwhile, the legend pays its way.

175
00:12:38,317 --> 00:12:48,533
Near the highway stands a monument to the aliens, complete with a mock-up satellite dish, and a sign at the lake calls the place the alien relic site.

176
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:53,124
Some say the quiet after two thousand two proves a cover-up.

177
00:12:53,124 --> 00:12:55,117
But the follow-up did happen.

178
00:12:55,117 --> 00:12:56,492
It found tree roots.

179
00:12:56,492 --> 00:12:58,966
And tree roots don't make headlines.

180
00:12:59,433 --> 00:13:01,133
Time for the case scorecard.

181
00:13:01,600 --> 00:13:04,674
Built by alien visitors: no evidence.

182
00:13:04,674 --> 00:13:07,831
Smelted by a lost Iron Age: ruled out.

183
00:13:07,831 --> 00:13:10,740
Modern scrap, or a hoax: ruled out.

184
00:13:10,740 --> 00:13:13,066
Formed from magma: unlikely.

185
00:13:13,533 --> 00:13:16,960
Iron and pyrite from flood sediment: evidence.

186
00:13:16,960 --> 00:13:19,865
Shape from fossil tree roots: evidence.

187
00:13:19,865 --> 00:13:23,366
The uranium, and the full lab data: still open.

188
00:13:23,833 --> 00:13:24,659
The verdict.

189
00:13:24,659 --> 00:13:28,514
The Baigong pipes are real, and they really are strange.

190
00:13:28,514 --> 00:13:30,167
But they were not built.

191
00:13:30,167 --> 00:13:30,855
They grew.

192
00:13:30,855 --> 00:13:37,533
Fossil roots of a lost forest, filled with iron by ancient floods, and lifted into a desert hill.

193
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:42,278
The most alien thing about them is the world they come from.

194
00:13:42,278 --> 00:13:45,772
A green, wet Qaidam Basin that no human ever saw.

195
00:13:45,772 --> 00:13:49,266
That is the real mystery these pipes are holding.

196
00:13:49,733 --> 00:13:50,440
Case closed?

197
00:13:50,440 --> 00:13:57,866
Tell us in the comments which suspect you would have picked, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery we put in the line-up.
